1.2  Conclusions
– These are four business models, not four comparables. Tata Power is an integrated utility carrying thermal generation and four Odisha distribution companies. Adani Green and NTPC Green are independent power producers converting debt into megawatts. Suzlon sells turbines to all of them. Every multiple in this report has to be read against that.
– Only one company is growing without leverage. Suzlon grew revenue 53.7% while holding a net cash position of ₹2,384 crore, because it does not own the fleet it builds. The two IPPs added ₹41,840 crore of net debt between them in a single year to fund capacity that has not yet reached the income statement.
– Adani Green's EBITDA rose 23% while profit after tax fell 15%. Interest and depreciation on ₹30,365 crore of FY2025-26 capital expenditure absorbed the entire operating gain. This is the clearest illustration in the peer set of why EBITDA alone is an inadequate lens on a capital-intensive IPP.
– Tata Power's revenue decline is a mix event, not a demand event. The Mundra plant was suspended for overhaul from 3 July 2025. Revenue fell 4.7%, yet EBITDA grew 11% because the earnings mix shifted decisively toward renewables, transmission and distribution — the renewables segment alone went from ₹9,876 crore to ₹15,028 crore.
– Valuation dispersion is four-fold and is explained by purity, not profitability. The set trades between 10.7x and 44.8x EV/EBITDA on a single day. NTPC Green's 2.8% return on equity supports a 155x earnings multiple because the market is pricing capacity it has not yet commissioned.
– Enterprise value per megawatt inverts the valuation ranking. NTPC Green is the cheapest name in the set per operating megawatt at ₹11.6 crore, against Adani Green at ₹16.6 crore, despite carrying the highest EV/EBITDA. The two measures disagree because one fleet is earning and the other is still being built.
– One of the four has no third-party ESG rating at all. NTPC Green carries an ₹81,162 crore market capitalisation and a 100% renewable revenue base, and no agency covered it as at 28 July 2026. For a mandate with a hard ESG screen this is a live admissibility question, not a footnote.
