2.2  Why this matters for every number that follows
Treating these four as substitutes produces conclusions that are arithmetically correct and analytically worthless. Two examples make the point.
EV/Revenue. Adani Green trades at 20.2x EV/Revenue and Suzlon at 4.5x. That gap says nothing about relative value: Adani Green books only power-supply revenue against a 94% margin, while Suzlon books equipment and EPC sales at an 18% margin. The multiple is measuring the revenue definition, not the business.
EBITDA margin. Adani Green's 93.6% and Suzlon's 18.1% are both entirely normal for what they do. A contracted IPP with no fuel cost and outsourced O&M should run above 90%. A manufacturer with a bill of materials should not.
The measures that do travel across this set are revenue growth, net debt to EBITDA, return on equity, EV/EBITDA and — between the two IPPs specifically — enterprise value per operating megawatt. Those are the measures this report leads with.
