3.2  Tata Power — a mix shift dressed as a decline
Tata Power is the only company in the set whose revenue fell in FY2025-26, and the headline is misleading. The Mundra ultra mega power plant was suspended for overhaul with effect from 3 July 2025, which removed roughly ₹9,000 crore from standalone revenue: standalone revenue from operations dropped from ₹22,359 crore to ₹13,226 crore and standalone profit from ₹3,133 crore to ₹1,125 crore.
Consolidated EBITDA nonetheless rose 11% to ₹16,090 crore, and consolidated profit after tax reached ₹5,118 crore — the first time the company has cleared ₹5,000 crore. The reason is composition. The renewables segment grew from ₹9,876 crore to ₹15,028 crore of revenue. Transmission and distribution profit rose 49% to ₹2,978 crore. The Odisha discoms delivered ₹809 crore, up 84%. Solar cell and module manufacturing profit doubled to ₹857 crore on yields above 95%.
A supplementary power purchase agreement with Gujarat Urja Vikas Nigam was executed on 23 March 2026 with effect from 1 April 2025, and the Ministry of Power has permitted operation under revised terms. Mundra's contribution should normalise in FY2026-27, which makes the FY2025-26 revenue line a poor base for any forward projection.

3.3  Adani Green — the divergence between EBITDA and profit
Adani Green added 5,051 MW of greenfield capacity in FY2025-26, 1.5 times the prior year and one of the largest annual additions anywhere outside China. Operating capacity rose 35% to 19,294 MW, energy sales rose 34% to 37,567 million units, revenue from power supply rose 22% and EBITDA rose 23%.
Profit after tax fell 15%, to approximately ₹1,570 crore. The company posted a loss of ₹41 crore in the December 2025 quarter. The entire operating gain was absorbed by interest and depreciation on ₹30,365 crore of capital expenditure. Management also estimates that grid curtailment and merchant-price realisation cost the company ₹1,200 to ₹1,500 crore of FY2025-26 EBITDA — roughly ₹500 crore from transmission constraints and ₹800 to ₹1,000 crore from selling power on the open market below contracted tariffs, a decision taken to secure transmission-charge waivers before they expired.
This is the single most important reading exercise in the report. A reader looking only at EBITDA sees a company compounding at 23%. A reader looking at the full income statement sees a company whose growth is currently being consumed by its own funding cost.

3.4  NTPC Green — asset growth ahead of earnings
NTPC Green grew revenue 29.3% to ₹2,858 crore, the second-fastest rate in the set, but EBITDA grew only 13.8% and profit after tax 9.9%. Earnings per share actually fell, from ₹0.67 to ₹0.62.
The reason is visible on the balance sheet rather than the income statement. Total assets rose from ₹45,421 crore to ₹60,381 crore, with over ₹15,264 crore spent on property, plant and equipment during the year. Finance costs of ₹887 crore for the year consumed a substantial share of operating profit. In the March 2026 quarter, revenue rose 46.7% year on year while net profit fell 15.4%, as finance costs jumped from ₹177 crore to ₹257 crore.
The company also lost 314 million units to grid curtailment during the year, with an estimated EBITDA impact of around ₹90 crore.

3.5  Suzlon — growth, and a question about its quality
Suzlon delivered the strongest operating year in the set on every growth measure: revenue up 53.7% to ₹16,679 crore, EBITDA up 63% to ₹3,022 crore, and margin expansion of 100 basis points to 18.1%. India deliveries of 2,456 MW were the highest in the company's history, and the order book closed at 5.9 GW with 66% from PSU and commercial and industrial customers.
The reported profit after tax of ₹3,163 crore requires a caveat. It includes a ₹742 crore deferred tax asset recognition. Profit before tax was ₹2,422 crore, up 67% — that is the figure to quote and the one on which any forward multiple should be built. On reported PAT, Suzlon's profit exceeds its EBITDA, which is not a sustainable relationship.
Working capital is the other point of attention. Consolidated cash and cash equivalents closed at ₹1,246 crore against ₹1,113 crore a year earlier, on revenue that grew by more than half. 971 MW was erected and awaiting commissioning at year end against 744 MW commissioned during the year, which is a meaningful quantum of revenue recognised ahead of cash conversion.

