4.2  Adani Green — the trade, stated plainly
Adani Green added ₹26,790 crore of net debt in a single year, taking net debt from ₹64,462 crore to ₹91,252 crore. That increment alone is roughly two and a half times the company's annual EBITDA. It funded ₹30,365 crore of capital expenditure and 5.1 GW of new capacity.
Against ₹19,965 crore of equity, net debt to equity stands at 4.57x — by a wide margin the most leveraged balance sheet in the set. Net debt to EBITDA of 8.4x on reported FY2025-26 EBITDA moderates to roughly 7.8x on an annualised run-rate basis, because a substantial share of the 5.1 GW was commissioned late in the year and has not yet contributed a full period of earnings. Neither figure is comfortable.
The bull case is that this is exactly what a growth IPP balance sheet should look like mid-build, that the assets are contracted under long-term PPAs at a 91% to 94% margin, and that leverage will de-gear as capacity seasons. The bear case is that the company is already producing a loss-making quarter at this level of gearing, and that the FY2026-27 target of more than 10,000 MWh of battery storage implies the capital cycle is not slowing.

4.3  NTPC Green — the highest ratio, the cheapest funding
NTPC Green shows the highest net debt to EBITDA in the set at 11.9x. Debt-to-equity climbed from 0.97x to 1.54x in twelve months, non-current borrowings rose from ₹17,301 crore to ₹28,637 crore, and total assets grew by a third.
Its mitigant is funding cost rather than balance sheet capacity. In July 2026 the company placed ₹2,500 crore of ten-year unsecured non-convertible debentures at 7.27%. A private independent power producer with a 1.54x debt-to-equity ratio and a 2.8% return on equity would not price anywhere near that. What is being financed here is partly the asset and partly the Maharatna parent, and the board has approved a further ₹5,000 crore borrowing programme for FY2026-27.

4.4  Tata Power — conventional, and drifting
At 1.18x net debt to equity and 3.2x net debt to EBITDA, Tata Power holds the most conventional balance sheet in the set. The direction of travel is worth noting: net debt to equity has moved from 1.05x to 1.18x over the year as clean-energy capital expenditure absorbed 62% of a five-year ₹33,000 crore programme.
The company also carries an unresolved contingency. A Singapore International Arbitration Centre award of USD 490.32 million remains under appeal. It is not provided for in these figures and is not reflected in any multiple in this report.

4.5  Suzlon — net cash is structural, not conservatism
Suzlon closed the year with a net cash position of ₹2,384 crore against a net worth of ₹9,464 crore. This should not be read as balance-sheet discipline relative to the IPPs. Suzlon does not own the generating fleet, so it does not carry the fleet's debt. That is the asset-light bargain, and it comes with the corresponding exposure to order-book timing rather than to interest rates.
The relevant balance-sheet risk for Suzlon is working capital, not leverage: rapid revenue growth on modest cash generation, with 971 MW erected and awaiting commissioning at year end.

