6.2  Where the dispersion comes from
The peer set trades between 10.7x and 44.8x EV/EBITDA — a four-fold range, inside one sector, on one day. Almost none of that dispersion is explained by profitability. It is explained by purity of exposure and by duration.
Tata Power is priced as a utility at 10.7x because it is one. Roughly three-quarters of its revenue comes from something other than renewables, and it carries thermal generation, four distribution companies and a manufacturing business. The market is not paying a renewable multiple for a renewable-adjacent business, and on a 23.4x earnings multiple with a 0.67% dividend yield it is not being asked to.
NTPC Green is priced on capacity it has not yet commissioned. That is how a 2.8% return on equity supports 155.5x earnings and 44.8x EBITDA. The multiple is not a statement about current earnings; it is a discount rate applied to a commissioning schedule.
Suzlon at 23.2x EV/EBITDA and 22.9x earnings is priced as a cyclical manufacturer despite a 5.9 GW order book and the highest return on equity in the set. Order books convert at the market's pace, not the company's, and the market prices that timing risk.

6.3  Enterprise value per megawatt inverts the ranking
For the two pure-play IPPs, enterprise value per operating megawatt is the cleanest like-for-like comparison available, and it reverses what EV/EBITDA suggests.
NTPC Green is the cheapest name in the set per operating megawatt at ₹11.6 crore, against Adani Green at ₹16.6 crore — despite carrying an EV/EBITDA more than half again as high. The two measures disagree because Adani Green's fleet is earning and NTPC Green's is still being built. Which measure is right depends entirely on whether a buyer underwrites NTPC Green's commissioning schedule. If the schedule delivers, the EBITDA multiple compresses toward the asset multiple. If it slips, the asset multiple was never the relevant one.
Tata Power's ₹21.7 crore per megawatt is shown for completeness and should not be compared. Its enterprise value also buys thermal generation, transmission and distribution, so dividing the whole by renewable capacity alone materially overstates the figure.

