7.2  One of the four has no rating at all
NTPC Green carries an ₹81,162 crore market capitalisation and a 100% renewable revenue base, and no third-party ESG rating could be located for it as at 28 July 2026. Newly listed public-sector subsidiaries routinely lag their parents into the ratings universe, and the company listed only in November 2024.
For a mandate operating a hard ESG screen this is not a footnote. An unrated holding can be inadmissible regardless of how green the underlying assets are, and the absence of a rating is not a neutral fact — it removes the company from consideration by exactly the pool of capital its business model is designed to attract. It is also the single easiest item on this list to resolve: the underlying model has an input cell waiting for the score, and every visual repopulates the day it publishes.

7.3  The company with the most coverage scores the worst
Tata Power is the only company in the set covered by three agencies, and it posts the lowest composite at 49.3. The driver is a Sustainalytics risk score of 41, which normalises to 18 out of 100 and reflects unmanaged risk from coal and thermal legacy assets. Its LSEG score of 73 shows the disclosure itself is strong.
Coverage and score are different things, and this is the clearest illustration of it in the set. Tata Power has the most evidence against it and the least flattering result. Adani Green has two agencies and the highest composite. Suzlon has one. A reader comparing 70.0 against 49.3 is not comparing like with like, which is why the agency count is presented alongside the score on every page of the underlying dashboard rather than buried in a note.

