8.3  What would change the view
– Adani Green: evidence that leverage has peaked. A year in which net debt grows more slowly than EBITDA would materially change the case. FY2026-27 first-quarter profit of ₹983 crore, up 19.3%, is an encouraging start.
– NTPC Green: commissioned capacity converting to earnings. The company moved from 8,478 MW commercial in January 2026 to 10,787 MW in July 2026. If return on equity does not begin to rise through FY2026-27, the duration argument weakens sharply.
– Tata Power: Mundra normalisation under the revised GUVNL supplementary PPA, and whether the renewables and Odisha momentum persists once the thermal base is restored.
– Suzlon: order-book conversion and cash generation. Revenue grew 54% while cash and equivalents grew 12%; that gap is the number to watch.
– All four: grid and transmission constraints. Curtailment cost Adani Green an estimated ₹500 crore and NTPC Green around ₹90 crore of FY2025-26 EBITDA. This is a sector-level risk that scales with installed capacity.
